%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 147873241f4f7964690daffb230f2761, type: 3}
  m_Name: Level2_TheInvestigation
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  levelId: level_2
  levelNumber: 2
  displayName: The Investigation
  sceneName: Room2_InvestigationOffice
  description: Flagged messages arrive in the investigation office. Inspect the sender,
    context and requests before you decide.
  previewImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 505a3627d9fec30428c0950dace725d0, type: 3}
  briefingTitle: 'LEVEL 2: THE INVESTIGATION'
  briefingText: Welcome to the investigation office. Several messages have been flagged
    for review. Some contain subtle warning signs, while others are legitimate communications.
    Examine the sender, context, wording, and requested actions before making your
    decision. Your accuracy and response time will affect your score.
  caseNotes:
  - During setup you ordered office supplies from Northstar Office Supplies through
    your customer account.
  - This morning you attended a project meeting led by Daniel Reed, the project coordinator.
  - You requested a password change for your Example Cloud Service account.
  - This morning the company announced a scheduled password change, due by Friday.
  objectives:
  - Review seven messages.
  - Classify each as SCAM or LEGITIMATE.
  - Investigate suspicious details before answering.
  - Earn a score based on accuracy and response speed.
  messages:
  - id: l2_updated_invoice
    channel: 1
    sender: "Billing Department \u2014 Northstar Office Supplies"
    senderDetail: billing@northstar-supplies.example
    subject: Updated Invoice Available
    body: Hello, your recent office supply order has an updated invoice reflecting
      the final shipping cost. Please review the invoice in your existing customer
      account. If you have questions about the amount, contact our billing team through
      the official website.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 1
    scenarioContext: The player previously placed an office supply order during the
      simulated setup. The communication matches that transaction and directs the
      player to an existing account rather than an unfamiliar attachment or link.
    explanation: The message matches a transaction you initiated and provides a safer
      route through your existing account. In real situations, verify the order and
      sender independently before trusting a billing message.
    trigger: 2
    requestedAction: Review the invoice in your existing customer account.
    linkNote: No link or attachment. It points to your existing account and the official
      website.
    clues:
    - Refers to a recent office supply order.
    - Questions are directed to the billing team through the official website.
    inspectables:
    - id: address
      field: 1
      match: 
      label: Sender address
      finding: billing@northstar-supplies.example - the supplier you really ordered
        from.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: domain
    - id: order
      field: 3
      match: your recent office supply order
      label: Reference
      finding: It refers to the office supply order you placed.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: matches
    - id: cost
      field: 3
      match: updated invoice reflecting the final shipping cost
      label: Money
      finding: It mentions an updated cost.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: money
    - id: account
      field: 3
      match: review the invoice in your existing customer account
      label: Where to look
      finding: It points you to your existing account - no attachment or unfamiliar
        link.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: account
    - id: contact
      field: 3
      match: contact our billing team through the official website
      label: Contact
      finding: Questions go through the official website.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: official
    evidence:
    - id: domain
      title: Supplier's own address
      whyItMatters: It comes from the supplier you actually use.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: matches
      title: Matches your real order
      whyItMatters: You did place an office supply order.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: account
      title: Points to your existing account
      whyItMatters: No attachment or link - you check it yourself.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: official
      title: Official website for questions
      whyItMatters: Real companies direct you to their own channels.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: money
      title: Mentions money
      whyItMatters: Invoices mention costs - on its own that isn't a warning sign.
      supportsVerdict: 0
  - id: l2_account_suspension
    channel: 0
    sender: Unknown Number
    senderDetail: +1 (555) 019-4471
    subject: 
    body: Your account will be suspended in 30 minutes due to unusual activity. Confirm
      your identity immediately through secure-check.example.invalid to prevent permanent
      restriction.
    link: secure-check.example.invalid
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: The message creates extreme urgency, uses an unknown sender, and
      directs you to an unfamiliar domain. Do not enter account credentials through
      the message. Open the official application or contact the provider independently.
    trigger: 1
    requestedAction: Confirm your identity on a website within 30 minutes.
    linkNote: secure-check.example.invalid (training placeholder, not opened). The
      domain does not name any provider.
    clues:
    - The number is not saved in your contacts.
    - It does not say which account or provider it is about.
    - 'Deadline: 30 minutes.'
    inspectables:
    - id: number
      field: 1
      match: 
      label: Phone number
      finding: +1 (555) 019-4471 isn't in your contacts - an ordinary mobile number.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: number
    - id: which
      field: 3
      match: Your account
      label: Which account?
      finding: It never says which account or which provider.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: vague
    - id: deadline
      field: 3
      match: suspended in 30 minutes
      label: Deadline
      finding: Only 30 minutes - pressure to act without checking.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: deadline
    - id: unusual
      field: 3
      match: unusual activity
      label: Reason given
      finding: '''Unusual activity'' sounds technical.'
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: unusual
    - id: identity
      field: 3
      match: Confirm your identity immediately
      label: What it asks
      finding: Asks you to confirm your identity on a website - a common way to steal
        logins.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: identity
    - id: link
      field: 4
      match: 
      label: Link
      finding: secure-check.example.invalid doesn't name any provider at all.
      linkAddress: https://secure-check.example.invalid/verify
      clueId: link
    evidence:
    - id: number
      title: Unknown number
      whyItMatters: The text didn't come from any service you recognise.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: vague
      title: No account or provider named
      whyItMatters: Real alerts say which account they mean.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: deadline
      title: 30-minute deadline
      whyItMatters: Extreme urgency is a pressure tactic.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: identity
      title: Asks you to confirm your identity via a link
      whyItMatters: This is how login details are stolen.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: link
      title: Link with no provider name
      whyItMatters: A real provider's link names the provider.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: unusual
      title: '''Unusual activity'''
      whyItMatters: Real alerts mention this too - on its own it isn't proof.
      supportsVerdict: 0
  - id: l2_project_schedule
    channel: 1
    sender: "Daniel Reed \u2014 Project Coordinator"
    senderDetail: daniel.reed@yourcompany.example
    subject: Updated Project Schedule
    body: Hi, I've updated the project schedule following our meeting. You can review
      the latest version in the team's usual shared workspace. Please let me know
      if any of the dates conflict with your availability.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 1
    scenarioContext: The player attended a simulated meeting earlier. The sender
      and subject match that context, and the message directs the player to the team's
      established workspace.
    explanation: The sender, topic, and timing match an expected work activity, and
      the message uses an established workspace rather than an unfamiliar link. Still
      verify the sender's identity and context if anything seems unusual.
    trigger: 2
    requestedAction: Review the schedule in the team's usual shared workspace and
      reply about date conflicts.
    linkNote: No link. It refers to the workspace your team already uses.
    clues:
    - Sent from a company address.
    - Refers to a meeting that happened today.
    inspectables:
    - id: address
      field: 1
      match: 
      label: Sender address
      finding: daniel.reed@yourcompany.example - a company address from a colleague
        you know.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: company
    - id: meeting
      field: 3
      match: following our meeting
      label: Context
      finding: It refers to the meeting you attended today.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: meeting
    - id: workspace
      field: 3
      match: the team's usual shared workspace
      label: Where to look
      finding: It uses the workspace your team already uses - no new link.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: workspace
    - id: dates
      field: 3
      match: let me know if any of the dates conflict
      label: What it asks
      finding: Only asks you to reply about dates - nothing sensitive.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: noask
    evidence:
    - id: company
      title: Company address of a known colleague
      whyItMatters: Sent from your company's own domain.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: meeting
      title: Matches today's meeting
      whyItMatters: The context fits what really happened.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: workspace
      title: Uses the usual shared workspace
      whyItMatters: No unfamiliar link or attachment.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: noask
      title: Asks for nothing sensitive
      whyItMatters: Only a reply about dates.
      supportsVerdict: 1
  - id: l2_gift_card_request
    channel: 1
    sender: Office Director
    senderDetail: office.director.desk@quickmail.example
    subject: "Confidential Request \u2014 Immediate Action"
    body: I'm currently occupied and need you to purchase six gift cards for an urgent
      client matter. Send me the redemption codes as soon as possible. Please keep
      this confidential until I return.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: The request uses authority, urgency, secrecy, and an unusual payment
      method to pressure the recipient. Verify the request with the director through
      a separate, established communication channel before taking action.
    trigger: 2
    requestedAction: Buy six gift cards and email the redemption codes.
    linkNote: No link.
    clues:
    - The sender is a job title with no personal name.
    - Sent from an external email service, not the company domain.
    - Asks you to keep it confidential.
    inspectables:
    - id: title
      field: 0
      match: 
      label: Sender name
      finding: Just a job title - no personal name.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: title
    - id: address
      field: 1
      match: 
      label: Sender address
      finding: quickmail.example is an outside email service, not your company's
        domain.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: external
    - id: busy
      field: 3
      match: I'm currently occupied
      label: Excuse
      finding: Says they're busy.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: busy
    - id: cards
      field: 3
      match: purchase six gift cards
      label: The request
      finding: Gift cards work like cash and can't be traced - a classic boss-impersonation
        request.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: cards
    - id: codes
      field: 3
      match: Send me the redemption codes as soon as possible
      label: Urgency
      finding: Wants the codes as soon as possible.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: urgency
    - id: secret
      field: 3
      match: keep this confidential
      label: Secrecy
      finding: Asks you to keep it secret, so no one can warn you.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: secret
    evidence:
    - id: title
      title: Job title instead of a name
      whyItMatters: Impersonators hide behind a title.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: external
      title: Outside email service, not the company domain
      whyItMatters: A real director writes from the company address.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: cards
      title: Asks for gift cards
      whyItMatters: Gift card codes are untraceable cash.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: urgency
      title: Wants the codes as soon as possible
      whyItMatters: Urgency stops you from checking.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: secret
      title: Asks for secrecy
      whyItMatters: Secrecy cuts you off from anyone who could warn you.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: busy
      title: '''I''m currently occupied'''
      whyItMatters: Managers really are busy - on its own this isn't proof (though
        it's a common excuse to avoid a call).
      supportsVerdict: 0
  - id: l2_password_changed
    channel: 1
    sender: Example Cloud Service
    senderDetail: no-reply@examplecloud.example
    subject: 'Security Notification: Password Changed'
    body: The password change you requested has been completed. If you made this
      change, no further action is needed. If you did not request it, open the official
      application directly and review your account security.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 1
    scenarioContext: The player previously requested a password change during the
      simulated setup.
    explanation: The message matches an action you requested and gives you a safe
      way to check your account. If you did not make the change, use the official
      application rather than links in unexpected messages.
    trigger: 2
    requestedAction: Nothing if you made the change; otherwise review security in
      the official app.
    linkNote: No link.
    clues:
    - Refers to a password change.
    - Suggests opening the official application yourself.
    inspectables:
    - id: address
      field: 1
      match: 
      label: Sender address
      finding: no-reply@examplecloud.example - the cloud service's own domain.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: domain
    - id: subject
      field: 2
      match: Security Notification
      label: Subject line
      finding: '''Security Notification'' can sound alarming.'
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: alarm
    - id: requested
      field: 3
      match: The password change you requested
      label: What it refers to
      finding: It refers to the password change you really made.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: matches
    - id: noaction
      field: 3
      match: no further action is needed
      label: What it asks
      finding: Nothing - no link, code or password.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: noask
    - id: official
      field: 3
      match: open the official application directly
      label: Advice
      finding: If it wasn't you, it tells you to open the official app yourself.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: official
    evidence:
    - id: domain
      title: The service's own domain
      whyItMatters: Sent from examplecloud.example, the real service.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: matches
      title: Matches the change you made
      whyItMatters: You did request a password change.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: noask
      title: Asks for nothing
      whyItMatters: No link, code or password is requested.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: official
      title: Tells you to use the official app
      whyItMatters: Genuine services send you to their own app.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: alarm
      title: '''Security Notification'' subject'
      whyItMatters: It sounds alarming, but it's a plain notice with no threat or
        deadline.
      supportsVerdict: 0
  - id: l2_it_support
    channel: 0
    sender: IT Helpdesk
    senderDetail: +1 (555) 016-2208
    subject: 
    body: We are updating employee security records. Reply with your account password
      and verification code so we can finish configuring your access before the end
      of the day.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: The message requests a password and verification code, which are
      sensitive authentication details. Legitimate support staff should not ask you
      to disclose your password or one-time verification code. Contact IT through
      its established support channel.
    trigger: 1
    requestedAction: Reply by text with your account password and verification code.
    linkNote: No link. The reply goes to this phone number.
    clues:
    - Sent from an ordinary phone number.
    - 'Deadline: before the end of the day.'
    inspectables:
    - id: name
      field: 0
      match: 
      label: Sender name
      finding: Calls itself 'IT Helpdesk' - anyone can set a display name.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
    - id: number
      field: 1
      match: 
      label: Phone number
      finding: +1 (555) 016-2208 is an ordinary phone number, not your company's
        IT system.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: number
    - id: records
      field: 3
      match: updating employee security records
      label: Reason given
      finding: Sounds official.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: records
    - id: password
      field: 3
      match: Reply with your account password and verification code
      label: What it asks
      finding: It asks for your password AND verification code by text. Real IT never
        asks for these.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: password
    - id: deadline
      field: 3
      match: before the end of the day
      label: Deadline
      finding: Pressure to do it today.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: deadline
    evidence:
    - id: password
      title: Asks for your password and verification code
      whyItMatters: No real IT team ever asks for these.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: number
      title: Ordinary phone number
      whyItMatters: Company IT doesn't text from random numbers.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: deadline
      title: '''Before the end of the day'''
      whyItMatters: A deadline to rush you.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: records
      title: Official-sounding reason
      whyItMatters: Anyone can claim to be 'updating records' - on its own it isn't
        evidence.
      supportsVerdict: 0
  - id: l2_it_chat
    channel: 4
    sender: "Priya Shah \u2014 IT Service Desk"
    senderDetail: "@priya.shah \xB7 verified \xB7 company directory"
    subject: 
    body: 'Priya: Hi! Reminder from the IT Service Desk - the scheduled password
      change from this morning''s company announcement is due by Friday.

      Priya:
      Please change it yourself in the IT portal from your bookmarks. Never share
      your password with anyone, including IT.

      You: Thanks, will do.

      Priya:
      If anything looks odd, call the service desk on extension 4100.'
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 1
    scenarioContext: This morning the company announced a scheduled password change,
      due by Friday.
    explanation: LEGITIMATE. The chat comes from a verified account in your company
      directory, matches this morning's announcement, asks you to change your password
      yourself in the IT portal (no link), warns you never to share it and offers
      a phone check. A deadline that matches an announced schedule isn't a scam sign
      by itself.
    trigger: 0
    requestedAction: Change your password yourself in the IT portal from your bookmarks,
      by Friday.
    linkNote: No link. It tells you to use the IT portal from your own bookmarks.
    clues:
    - Sent from a verified account in the company directory.
    - Refers to this morning's company announcement.
    inspectables:
    - id: account
      field: 1
      match: 
      label: Account
      finding: A verified account listed in your company directory.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: verified
    - id: announce
      field: 3
      match: the scheduled password change from this morning's company announcement
      label: Context
      finding: It matches this morning's real company announcement.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: matches
    - id: friday
      field: 3
      match: due by Friday
      label: Deadline
      finding: A deadline several days away.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: friday
    - id: portal
      field: 3
      match: change it yourself in the IT portal from your bookmarks
      label: How to do it
      finding: No link to click - you go to the portal yourself.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: portal
    - id: never
      field: 3
      match: Never share your password with anyone, including IT
      label: Password advice
      finding: It warns you never to share your password - the opposite of a phisher.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: nopassword
    - id: call
      field: 3
      match: call the service desk on extension 4100
      label: Contact
      finding: It offers a phone check.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: phone
    evidence:
    - id: verified
      title: Verified internal account
      whyItMatters: The chat comes from a verified account in your directory.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: matches
      title: Matches the company announcement
      whyItMatters: It repeats this morning's official notice.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: portal
      title: No link - go to the portal yourself
      whyItMatters: Typing or bookmarking the official portal avoids fake links.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: nopassword
      title: Never asks for your password
      whyItMatters: It tells you not to share it with anyone.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: phone
      title: Offers a phone check
      whyItMatters: Inviting a call makes it easy to verify - scammers avoid this.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: friday
      title: '''Due by Friday'''
      whyItMatters: A deadline days away that matches the announcement isn't pressure
        - on its own it isn't a scam sign.
      supportsVerdict: 0
  inboxExtras:
  - id: l2_old_order
    channel: 1
    sender: Northstar Office Supplies
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Order received
    body: Thanks for your order of printer paper, folders and pens. You can view
      the order status and invoice in your customer account.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 
    trigger: 0
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
  - id: l2_old_meeting
    channel: 1
    sender: Calendar
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Project meeting with Daniel Reed - 9:00
    body: 'Reminder: Project meeting with Daniel Reed (Project Coordinator), Room
      2B, 9:00-9:45.'
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 
    trigger: 0
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
  - id: l2_old_facilities
    channel: 1
    sender: Facilities Team
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Air conditioning maintenance on Friday
    body: Technicians will service the air conditioning on Friday morning. Please
      keep the server room door clear.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 
    trigger: 0
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
  caseFiles: []
  firstMessageDelay: 8
  delayBetweenMessages: {x: 10, y: 20}
  minimumActivityDelay: 4
  oneStarScore: 1
  twoStarScore: 715
  threeStarScore: 900
  minScoreToUnlockNext: 715
  scoring:
    basePoints: 150
    speedBonus: 15
    fastWindow: 0.35
    wrongPenalty: 25
    recoveryPoints: 40
    baseReadingSeconds: 20
    secondsPerWord: 0.5
    secondsPerDetail: 3
    minReadingSeconds: 40
    maxReadingSeconds: 120
    caseExtraSeconds: 60
    overtimeStepSeconds: 15
    overtimePenaltyPerStep: 5
    maxTimePenalty: 30
    minCorrectPoints: 120
  nextLevelId: level_3
  nextSceneName: Room3_DigitalTrap
  lessons:
  - 'Impersonation: names and job titles can be faked - check the actual address
    or number.'
  - 'Suspicious domains: a link''s domain should clearly match the organisation it
    claims to be.'
  - Urgency and secrecy are pressure tactics - slow down and verify through a separate
    channel.
  - Never share passwords, verification codes or gift card codes by message.
  - Messages that match your own recent actions and point to official channels are
    more likely legitimate.
  learningSummary: You learned to recognize impersonation, suspicious domains, urgent
    requests, unusual payment demands, credential theft attempts, and legitimate
    communications that match your own actions.
  performanceHigh: Excellent investigation! You identified the key warning signs
    and made efficient decisions.
  performanceModerate: Good work. Review the explanations to sharpen your judgment
    and improve your response time.
  performanceLow: Keep learning. Examine sender identity, context, urgency, and requests
    for sensitive information before deciding.
